  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Rotting trim on your log cabin can be a real headache, but addressing it promptly is key to preventing further damage. First things first, you've got to inspect all the trim around your windows, doors, and corners. You're looking for cracks, loose boards, obvious rot, and any signs of moisture getting in. Once you've identified the damaged sections, the next step is to carefully measure and remove them. It's super important to cut out all the compromised wood. Then, you'll need to cut and fit matching replacement pieces. This is where getting the right profile is crucial for a seamless look. Once the new pieces are in place, fasten them securely. After that, make sure to seal all the joints properly to prevent any future moisture intrusion. Finally, prime and paint or apply another appropriate finish to the repaired area. This isn't just for aesthetics; it's essential for weather protection. Remember, if the rot is minor, you might be able to get away with removing the soft wood and using an epoxy or filler. But if it's advanced, replacement is usually the best, most durable option. Don't let untreated trim rot linger, especially before the wet weather hits!"
    }
  ],
